  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION FOR 00Z TAF PACKAGE...\n\nTropical Cyclone Marie is about 800 miles south of San Diego, CA \nthis morning. A swath of high clouds and moisture from the cyclone \nhave made their way into Southern California. The only radar returns \nover SoCal as of 11 AM were in Imperial County. As Marie continues \nto track north and west this weekend, additional moisture will be \npushed into the area increasing chances of showers. The highest \nchances are tomorrow north of San Diego County. Total rainfall \namounts may reach over a half an inch over the San Bernardino \nMountains, and generally 0.20 to 0.30 inches for the Inland Empire, \nwith slightly lesser amounts to the coastal areas of Orange County, \ntapering off to lesser amounts over San Diego County. It's possible \nsome areas in southern San Diego County may not see any accumulating \nrainfall. There is a 5 to 10 percent chance of thunderstorms over \nthe San Bernardino Mountains and High Desert Sunday afternoon. Drier \nconditions expected for Monday and Tuesday. \n\nFor this weekend, high temperatures will remain below average inland \nwith highs near the coast running a few degrees above average. \nOvernight lows will also return to above average for this time of \nyear by Sunday. By Tuesday, high temperatures will be within a few \ndegrees of average for the mountains and deserts and 3 to 8 degrees \nabove average for the coast and valleys. Above average low \ntemperatures will be maintained into next weekend. Widespread minor \nto moderate HeatRisk is expected for inland areas Wednesday and \nThursday. Temperatures will peak on Wednesday and gradually cool \ninto Friday. \n\nThe upper level flow turns southeasterly Wednesday and Thursday, \nwhich may allow for some additional moisture to creep into the area. \nThere still remains some uncertainty in the upper level pattern with \nthe position of the upper level ridge over the southwestern US and a \ntrough off the US West Coast. A more easterly position of the trough \nwould result in drier conditions with a more westerly position of \nthe ridge allowing more moisture into the area. Current forecast \nfollows the NBM which has 20 to 40 percent chance of showers and a \n15 to 20 percent chance of thunderstorms over the mountains \nWednesday and Thursday afternoon.",
